   The stairs abruptly end at another set of black doors.  They are 
enormous, big enough for legions of armies to pass through, and their 
black surfaces are covered with ancient runes.
   The hot air makes you cough and choke, draining you of the last bit 
of energy you had.  As you stumble in the murk, your flesh and will 
finally give in.  As you crouch under the great doors, you think of 
your home.  At first the vision seems small and remote, as if you are 
seeing it through a looking glass.  However, the more and more you 
think about sky and grass and trees, the more present and tangible the 
memory becomes.
   And then a voice in your head -your voice, not the delusions of your 
fatigue or Melkor's taunting- tells you that must go on, you must see it 
through.  As your head clears, a new sense of purpose and resolve possesses 
you.  You know that even if you fail, there will always be some flower that 
grows, or some bird that sings, untouched by the evil of Melkor.  He will
not endure forever, for in the end the shadow is but a passing thing.
